00:13:19.640 --> 00:13:21.040
often in massive code base.

00:13:21.040 --> 00:13:24.480
So you need to harness the agents
to actually know what the code is.

00:13:24.520 --> 00:13:26.520
You need to understand it.
They need to onboard themselves.

00:13:26.520 --> 00:13:29.520
They do not need to depend on a developer
to tell them what to do.

00:13:29.560 --> 00:13:31.680
They need to be able to communicate
with the user.

00:13:31.680 --> 00:13:33.360
You can't depend on a user to anchor

00:13:33.360 --> 00:13:35.840
and say in this file
and this span of lines, let's work on it.

00:13:35.840 --> 00:13:36.840
You need to understand

00:13:36.840 --> 00:13:40.200
the product level language and what that
actually translates into code.

00:13:40.240 --> 00:13:43.280
And they need to manage really long
sessions and keep the context fresh.

00:13:43.320 --> 00:13:44.640
They need to be able to check their own

00:13:44.640 --> 00:13:48.840
work to get automatic feedback
loops and higher quality.

00:13:48.840 --> 00:13:49.400
With that,

00:13:49.400 --> 00:13:52.760
we also need to know what good means
and of course they need to improve it.

00:13:52.760 --> 00:13:54.920
That's the key part of the harness.

00:13:54.920 --> 00:13:57.600
So we personally put a lot of emphasis
on all of this.

00:13:57.600 --> 00:14:00.760
But in particular when working
with non-technical individuals,

00:14:00.760 --> 00:14:04.040
you need somebody to own the code
and read the code that's on the agent.

00:14:04.040 --> 00:14:07.400
You don't want to translate
all those code constraints

00:14:07.400 --> 00:14:11.320
to the product every single time
you talk to them, you want to eat them.

00:14:11.320 --> 00:14:13.760
Just know that inherently and factor
it in.

00:14:13.760 --> 00:14:16.760
And you want the agent to check us off
constantly.

00:14:17.080 --> 00:14:20.080
There's nothing worse than that
overconfident agent.

00:14:20.080 --> 00:14:21.880
And finally, you want to learn

00:14:21.880 --> 00:14:25.680
not just from an individual session,
but from all of your users in parallel

00:14:25.680 --> 00:14:30.080
so that the growth is actually exponential
and truly accumulative.

00:14:30.960 --> 00:14:34.800
And we often get is that sounds amazing?

00:14:34.800 --> 00:14:36.120
But I just enough

00:14:36.120 --> 00:14:39.720
like my code is really difficult, setting
it up is not actually going to work.

00:14:40.000 --> 00:14:44.200
And the answer is, frankly,
we've never not been able to make it work.

00:14:44.200 --> 00:14:45.400
Everything is feasible.

00:14:45.400 --> 00:14:47.440
You just have to to go about it
the right way.

00:14:47.440 --> 00:14:49.440
Since we're talking about non-technical
individuals,

00:14:49.440 --> 00:14:51.240
take care of much more
about the user facing.

00:14:51.240 --> 00:14:54.480
So maybe if you're super complicated,
micro system

00:14:54.480 --> 00:14:57.880
architecture back in is not feasible
to set up for them every time.

00:14:58.040 --> 00:15:01.120
Focus more in front than we can
run setups

00:15:01.120 --> 00:15:04.160
than are crazy monopole multi repo

00:15:04.200 --> 00:15:07.560
with like sibling libraries to be compiled
so the agent can actually see it.

00:15:07.560 --> 00:15:10.560
And we've done with everything
in terms of security

00:15:10.560 --> 00:15:14.200
from like secrets to access
private repositories and artifacts

00:15:14.400 --> 00:15:18.600
that contain like the core libraries
that you need to actually run.

00:15:18.640 --> 00:15:21.240
So everything is feasible for
the agents can manage everything.

00:15:21.240 --> 00:15:24.400
Now, we've not seen a
case that is not manageable,

00:15:25.360 --> 00:15:27.840
but it is really important to say

00:15:27.840 --> 00:15:30.840
that people that care can't do work,

00:15:30.920 --> 00:15:33.600
that they're not really authorized to do.

00:15:33.600 --> 00:15:35.760
So there is very much a line in the sand.

00:15:35.760 --> 00:15:38.720
The line in the sand is engineers
most definitely have their place,

00:15:38.720 --> 00:15:42.320
but it's in making the judgment
calls on the actual engineering

00:15:42.320 --> 00:15:45.640
decisions and architecture decisions
that the DB, the back end,

00:15:46.120 --> 00:15:50.240
and having them focus again
on stuff that they care about more.

00:15:51.040 --> 00:15:55.040
Whereas the non-technical individuals
can focus on the user facing changes

00:15:55.200 --> 00:15:59.200
and whether that's tiny stuff like copy
to layout to new features,

00:15:59.200 --> 00:16:00.640
to getting new x ray.

00:16:00.640 --> 00:16:05.880
And what that actually goes down to is
they can do the user facing the UX work,

00:16:05.880 --> 00:16:09.000
and you get your engineers
to focus on what really matters of AP.

